  "summary": "Assembly theory, indel selection models and global Drosophila strain panels now quantify selection across genomes and molecules.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is selection as a quantifiable driver of genomic and molecular change. A Drosophila study assembles a high-quality D. simulans reference genome and integrates roughly 2,000 global strains to compare repeated adaptive evolution with D. melanogaster. A phytoplankton study derives a selection-coefficient model for indels, in which phosphorus and nitrogen costs predict genome streamlining under nutrient limitation. A cheminformatics study applies assembly theory to natural product assembly space, quantifying evolutionary selection and contingency in molecular evolution. What is new is the explicit quantification of selection: indel fitness costs tied to nutrient economy, adaptive convergence measured across a global strain panel, and an assembly-based measure of molecular evolutionary contingency. The items differ in organism and scale, so the shared element is a quantitative framework for inferring selection from sequence or molecular structure.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-09"
}
